        "content_type": "text/html",
        "body": "<a href=\"https://zammad.avicennaresearch.com/#user/profile/478\" data-mention-user-id=\"478\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer noopener\" title=\"https://zammad.avicennaresearch.com/#user/profile/478\">Sara Farnya</a> As for the questions:<div>1- What do they mean by signal to noise? Each GPS data has the following attributes: Lattitude, Longitude, precision, collection accuracy so the final result is like this:</div><div>(12.125487, 84.256398) -&gt; Coordinates</div><div>~5 (the coordinates are precise up to 5 meters)</div><div>High accuracy (the device used both the mobile network and satellite data to calculate the coordinate and accuracy)</div><div>And if the device is unable to calculate the coordinates, or the coordinates are off, there's practically no ground truth to understand the nature of the data. So I think the SNR question is meaningless. <a href=\"https://zammad.avicennaresearch.com/#user/profile/6\" data-mention-user-id=\"6\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer noopener\" title=\"https://zammad.avicennaresearch.com/#user/profile/6\">Mohammad Hashemian</a> please advise.</div><div>2- I actually discussed this subject in our geofencing meeting, I believe it shouldn't be that hard to implement as mobile devices collect the data by nature and we just need to store them (which is the logical thing to do as the data might be meaningless otherwise). However currently we don't have it. But I'll add it to the pipeline based on the client and contract's evaluation if it's beneficial.</div>",
